{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"toolcall_delta","contentIndex":2,"delta":"quote","partial":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"21 tests pass. Now let me activate the rounding test and integration tests.\n\nActually, I haven't tested the rounding behavior yet for premiums. Let me check what rounding test is left.\n\nLooking at my test list:\n```\ndescribe(\"Rounding\", () => {\n  it.todo(\"premium of 197.5 G rounds UP to 198 G (MHPCO's favor)\");\n});\n```\n\nI need a scenario where the premium comes out to exactly 197.5. Let me think... \n\nHmm, this is tricky because my customer is always newcomer or 2-year customer. Let me think about what combination gives exactly 197.5.\n\nFor the rounding test, I need a scenario where the calculation yields a fraction. Let me try:\n- 5 swords with a 1-year customer (no loyalty), 1 prior contract (follow-up applies)\n- base = 500\n- adjusted = 500 (no item modifiers)\n- first insurance = 10% × 500 × 5 = 250\n- follow-up = -15% × 500 = -75\n- Total: 500 + 250 - 75 + 5 = 680\n\nHmm, no fraction.\n\nLet me try:\n- 1 sword + 2 runes with a 1-year customer (no loyalty), 1 prior contract (follow-up)\n- base = 100 + 50 = 150 (raw) — but runes = 50 base premium, not block\n- adjusted total = 150\n- first insurance = 10% × 150 × 3 = 45\n- follow-up = -15% × 150 = -22.5\n- Total: 150 + 45 - 22.5 + 5 = 177.5 → ceil 178\n\nThat works! 177.5 → 178.\n\nOr maybe:\n- 3 amulets with 1-year customer, 0 prior contracts\n- base = 3 × 60 = 180\n- adjusted = 180\n- first insurance = 10% × 180 × 3 = 54\n- loyalty: 0 (1 year < 2)\n- follow-up: 0 (0 prior)\n- Total: 180 + 54 + 5 = 239\n\nNo fraction.\n\nLet me think about the spec example: \"a premium calculation that yields 197.5 G → final premium 198 G\". This is just an illustrative example.\n\nFor my test, I'll use a scenario that yields a fraction. Let me use:\n- 1 sword with 1-year customer, 1 prior contract\n- base = 100\n- adjusted = 100\n- first insurance = 10\n- loyalty: 0 (1 year)\n- follow-up = -15\n- Total: 100 + 10 - 15 + 5 = 100\n\nNo fraction.\n\n- 1 amulet with 1-year, 1 prior:\n- base = 60\n- adjusted = 60\n- first insurance = 6\n- follow-up = -9\n- Total: 60 + 6 - 9 + 5 = 62\n\nNo fraction.\n\nHmm. Let me try 2 amulets with 1-year, 1 prior:\n- base = 120\n- first insurance = 10% × 120 × 2 = 24\n- follow-up = -15% × 120 = -18\n- Total: 120 + 24 - 18 + 5 = 131\n\nNo fraction.\n\nNeed to find a combination that yields a fraction. The fractions come from:\n- first insurance (always 10% × base × numItems = whole × numItems / 10)\n- follow-up discount (-15% × base = base × 3 / 20)\n- loyalty discount (-20% × base = base × 1 / 5)\n\nFor 10% × base × numItems to be a fraction, base × numItems must not be divisible by 10. So base × numItems = 15, 25, 35, etc.\n\nFor 15% × base to be a fraction, base must not be divisible by 20. So base = 15, 35, 55, etc.\n\nFor 20% × base to be a fraction, base must not be divisible by 5. So base = 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 7, etc.\n\nSo I need a base that's not a multiple of 20 (to make loyalty/first insurance fractions), or not a multiple of 5 (to make loyalty fraction).\n\nFor 1-year customer (no loyalty), the fractions come from first insurance and follow-up.\n- first insurance: 10% × base × numItems. Fraction if base × numItems not divisible by 10.\n- follow-up: 15% × base. Fraction if base not divisible by 20.\n\nSo if base = 25 (1 rune) × 1 item = 25. First insurance = 2.5. Follow-up = 3.75. Total: 25 + 2.5 - 3.75 + 5 = 28.75 → ceil 29.\n\nLet me verify: 25 + 2.5 = 27.5, - 3.75 = 23.75, + 5 = 28.75. Ceil = 29.\n\nHmm but the spec example is 197.5 → 198. Let me see if I can construct that.\n\n197.5 = adjustedTotal + firstInsurance - loyaltyDiscount - followUpDiscount + 5\n\nSo adjustedTotal + firstInsurance - loyaltyDiscount - followUpDiscount = 192.5\n\nIf we set up the math to get 192.5, we'd have 192.5 + 5 = 197.5 → 198.\n\nLet me think about what combination gives that.\n\nActually the spec example \"197.5 → 198\" is just illustrative. My test can use a different value. Let me just use 1 rune with 1-year, 1 prior contract:\n- 25 + 2.5 - 3.75 + 5 = 28.75 → ceil 29\n\nActually let me use 3 runes with 1-year, 1 prior:\n- base = 75 (raw, no block... wait, 3 runes with block: base = 60)\n- adjusted = 60 (block applied)\n- first insurance = 10% × 60 × 3 = 18\n- follow-up = -15% × 60 = -9\n- Total: 60 + 18 - 9 + 5 = 74\n\nHmm no fraction.\n\nLet me think differently. Use 4 runes with 1-year, 1 prior:\n- base = 100 (no block)\n- first insurance = 40\n- follow-up = -15\n- Total: 100 + 40 - 15 + 5 = 130\n\nNo fraction.\n\nUse 2 amulets (base 120) with 1-year, 1 prior:\n- adjusted = 120\n- first insurance = 24\n- follow-up = -18\n- Total: 120 + 24 - 18 + 5 = 131\n\nNo fraction.\n\nHmm. Use 1 amulet (base 60) with 1-year, 1 prior:\n- adjusted = 60\n- first insurance = 6\n- follow-up = -9\n- Total: 60 + 6 - 9 + 5 = 62\n\nNo fraction.\n\nUse 2 potions (base 80) with 1-year, 1 prior:\n- adjusted = 80\n- first insurance = 16\n- follow-up = -12\n- Total: 80 + 16 - 12 + 5 = 89\n\nNo fraction.\n\nUse 1 sword enchantment 5 (base 100, adjusted 130) with 1-year, 1 prior:\n- adjusted = 130\n- first insurance = 10\n- follow-up = -15\n- Total: 130 + 10 - 15 + 5 = 130\n\nNo fraction.\n\nI need base that's not a multiple of 5 or 10 to get a fraction. Components are 25 each, so multiples of 25 are easy. With 1 rune: base 25, follow-up = 3.75. With 2 runes: base 50, follow-up = 7.5.\n\nLet me try 2 runes with 1-year, 1 prior:\n- adjusted = 50 (no block)\n- first insurance = 10% × 50 × 2 = 10\n- follow-up = -7.5\n- Total: 50 + 10 - 7.5 + 5 = 57.5 → ceil 58\n\nYes! This gives 57.5 → 58.\n\nLet me use this for the rounding test.\n\nActually wait, I want to be careful. Let me check the spec example values.\n\nThe spec says: \"a premium calculation that yields 197.5 G → final premium 198 G (rounded up)\". This is illustrative. My test can use any value that exercises the rounding.\n\nLet me use 2 runes with 1-year, 1 prior contract → 57.5 → 58.\n\nActually, I realize I want to test both halves of the rounding behavior:\n1. Premium fraction → round up (MHPCO's favor)\n2. Payout fraction → round down (MHPCO's favor)\n\nThe payout rounding is in claim.spec.ts. The premium rounding is here.\n\nLet me activate the test.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"## Red -- Test 22: premium rounding (197.5 → 198)"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_32873b80029841d0bd3ac483","name":"edit","arguments":{"edits":[{"newText":"    it(\"premium calculation that yields a fraction rounds UP (MHPCO's favor)\", () => {\n      // 2 runes + 1-year customer + 1 prior contract:\n      // base 50, first insurance 10, follow-up = -15% × 50 = -7.5\n      // Total: 50 + 10 - 7.5 + 5 = 57.5 → ceil 58\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 1, contractCount: 1 };\n      expect(calculatePremium(\n        [{ type: \"rune\" }, { type: \"rune\" }],\n        customer,\n      )).toBe(58);\n    });","oldText":"    it.todo(\"premium of 197.5 G rounds UP to 198 G (MHPCO's favor)\");"}],"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-24_21-00-58_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_minimax-m3/src/quote"},"partialArgs":"{\"edits\": [{\"newText\": \"    it(\\\"premium calculation that yields a fraction rounds UP (MHPCO's favor)\\\", () => {\\n      // 2 runes + 1-year customer + 1 prior contract:\\n      // base 50, first insurance 10, follow-up = -15% × 50 = -7.5\\n      // Total: 50 + 10 - 7.5 + 5 = 57.5 → ceil 58\\n      const customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: 1, contractCount: 1 };\\n      expect(calculatePremium(\\n        [{ type: \\\"rune\\\" }, { type: \\\"rune\\\" }],\\n        customer,\\n      )).toBe(58);\\n    });\", \"oldText\": \"    it.todo(\\\"premium of 197.5 G rounds UP to 198 G (MHPCO's favor)\\\");\"}], \"path\": \"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-24_21-00-58_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-